[2026-11-17T11:54:18.527Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'gh issue view https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/issues/62 --json number,title,body,comments,state,url && gh api repos/konard/voice-simulator/issues/62/comments --paginate'","aggregated_output":"{\"body\":\"> Part of the [roadmap](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/ROADMAP.md) planned in #11 (Roadmap 4: Reverse simulation (listening)). Type: **Feature**. Requirements of #11 covered: R6, R10, R12, R13, R18 (see the [case study](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/case-studies/issue-11/README.md)).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\n`findLandmarks` reads the analysis frames and says three things without knowing the language: where each syllable has its centre (nucleus), where the sound changes abruptly (landmarks: a closure, a release, the start or end of voicing), and which broad class every frame belongs to (silence, vowel, glide, nasal, voiced fricative, voiceless fricative, closure, burst). In a stream a nucleus is reported at most 150 ms after it was spoken.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n- Live highlighting (#74) must mark the syllable being spoken long before the words are known. Nuclei give that from energy alone.\\n- The decoder (#66) needs places where a sound may begin or end, and a cheap first guess of the kind of sound, so that it compares each stretch with a few phonemes and not with all 97.\\n- Speech rate for the voice fit (#63) is the number of nuclei per second.\\n\\n## Current state\\n\\n- `analyzeVoice` gives one number, `syllableRate`: peaks of a smoothed energy curve of the whole recording. The peak times are not returned and nothing works on a stream.\\n- No code finds closures, bursts or the start of voicing, and no code names the kind of sound of a frame.\\n- The forward direction knows all of this exactly: `planUtterance` returns `plan.segments` with `kind` (`pause`, `consonant`, `release`, `vowel`), `start` and `end`, and the tokens carry manner and voicing (`src/articulation/phones.js`, `MANNERS`). So the simulator can make as much labelled speech as needed to set thresholds.\\n- `syllabifyIpa(ipa, {language})` in `src/ipa/syllables.js` gives the true number of syllables of a transcript.\\n\\n## What to do\\n\\nCreate `src/recognition/landmarks.js` (stage pattern of #58) and the script `scripts/calibrate-landmarks.mjs`. Inputs per frame, all from the frame set: `energy`, `noise`, `speech`, `periodicity`, `zcr`, `gravity`, `tilt`, `band` (6 values) and `mel`.\\n\\n1. Exported constant `BROAD_CLASSES`, a list of names in this order (the index is the class number): `silence`, `vowel`, `glide`, `nasal`, `voiced-fricative`, `voiceless-fricative`, `closure`, `burst`.\\n2. Sonority curve `s[t]` in dB: `10 * log10` of the sum of `exp(mel[m])` over the filters with centres from 300 to 3000 Hz (to 2800 Hz when `rate` is 8000), smoothed with a moving mean of 40 ms. Store it as `sonority`.\\n3. Nuclei (after Mermelstein 1975; de Jong and Wempe 2009). Frame `t` is a nucleus when all hold:\\n   1. `s[t]` is the largest value within 40 ms on both sides;\\n   2. `s[t] >= noise[t] + NUCLEUS_ABOVE_NOISE` (start value 10 dB);\\n   3. `periodicity[t] >= 0.45` (voiced);\\n   4. between `t` and the nucleus before it `s` falls at least `NUCLEUS_DIP` (start value 2 dB) below the lower of the two peaks;\\n   5. the nucleus before it is at least 80 ms away. If rule 4 or 5 fails, keep the higher of the two peaks only.\\n\\n   A nucleus is confirmed, and never taken back, when `s` has fallen `NUCLEUS_DIP` below it or when 100 ms have passed, whichever comes first; with the 40 ms of rule 1 the delay is at most 150 ms. Each nucleus is `{time, start, end, strength}`: `strength` = `s[t]` minus the lower of the two minima around it; `start` and `end` = the minima of `s` between neighbouring nuclei, or the nearest frame with `speech` 0. `end` is `null` until the next minimum is known.\\n\\n4. Landmarks (after Liu 1996). For each of the six bands compute the rise `r_b[t] = band_b[t + d] - band_b[t - d]` with `d` = 25 ms (coarse) and 13 ms (fine). A peak of `|r_b|` above `LANDMARK_RISE` (start value 9 dB) in the coarse pass, placed at the nearest peak of the fine pass within 20 ms, is a candidate. Types:\\n   - `g+` / `g-`: band 0 (0 to 400 Hz) rises or falls and `periodicity` crosses 0.45: voicing starts or stops.\\n   - `s+` / `s-`: inside a voiced stretch, at least three of bands 1 to 4 rise or fall together: a sonorant consonant ends or begins.\\n   - `b+` / `b-`: outside a voiced stretch, at least three of bands 1 to 5 rise or fall together: a burst or frication starts or stops.\\n\\n   Each landmark is `{time, type, strength}` (`strength` = mean `|r_b|` in dB over the bands that took part). Two landmarks of one type closer than 20 ms: keep the stronger.\\n\\n5. Broad class of frame `t`, a fixed tree; `e = energy - noise`:\\n   1. `speech` is 0 and `e < T.silence` : silence (or closure, see step 6).\\n   2. `periodicity < T.voiced`: if the frame is within 20 ms after a `b+` landmark and the frame 20 ms earlier was silence or closure: burst; otherwise voiceless fricative.\\n   3. voiced, and `zcr > T.fricativeZcr` or `gravity > T.fricativeGravity`: voiced fricative.\\n   4. voiced, `band[0] - band[2] > T.nasalDrop` and `s[t] < peak - T.nasalBelowPeak`: nasal. `peak` = the largest `s` from 200 ms before the frame to 40 ms after it.\\n   5. voiced and `s[t] < peak - T.glideBelowPeak`: glide (this class also holds liquids).\\n   6. otherwise vowel.\\n\\n   In a stream the class of a frame is fixed 60 ms after the frame (40 ms for `peak`, 20 ms for the clean-up of step 6); `committed` counts these.\\n\\n6. Clean-up: a median over 5 frames of the class numbers; a silence run shorter than 150 ms with speech on both sides becomes closure; a run of any class shorter than 15 ms takes the class of the longer neighbour (bursts may be 5 ms).\\n7. `scripts/calibrate-landmarks.mjs` finds the thresholds `T` and the three start values above. It must be deterministic and is run by hand:\\n   1. Render with `synthesize` (`solver: 'waveguide'`, `seed: 1`) every consonant of `src/ipa/inventory.js` between the vowels `a`, `i`, `u` (`aCa`, `iCi`, `uCu`), for the presets male, female and child, at 8000, 11025 and 16000 Hz, clean and with seeded white noise 20 dB below the speech.\\n   2. Truth per frame from `plan.segments` and the token: [REDACTED] is silence; vowel is vowel; a consonant by manner: plosive closure part is closure, its `release` segment burst; nasal is nasal; fricatives by voicing; approximants, laterals, taps and trills are glide. Frames within 10 ms of a segment edge are not counted.\\n   3. Coordinate search: for each threshold in turn try 21 values across its allowed range, keep the one with the highest mean of the per-class recall; three rounds.\\n   4. Write `src/recognition/landmark-thresholds.js` (one exported object, with the reached recall per class and per rate in a comment).\\n8. Result: an object with `hop`, `startTime`, `count`, `committed`, `classes`, `sonority`, `landmarks` and `nuclei`; `classes` is a `Uint8Array`, `sonority` a `Float32Array`, both with `frames.capacity` values; `landmarks` and `nuclei` are arrays that only grow. Exports: `findLandmarks(frames, options)`, `BROAD_CLASSES` and `createLandmarkDetector(frames, options)` (its `advance()` returns the nuclei confirmed by this call).\\n\\nBudget on the reference machine of #58: at most 2 % of real time.\\n\\n## Tests\\n\\nNew file `tests/recognition-landmarks.test.js`. Sentences: \\\"The north wind and the sun were disputing which was the stronger.\\\" (English), \\\"Мама мыла раму.\\\" (Russian), \\\"नमस्ते दुनिया\\\" (Hindi), \\\"你好世界\\\" (Mandarin); presets male and female; 16000 and 8000 Hz; `lang` set for each. True nuclei = the middle of each vowel segment of `plan.segments`; vowel segments with no consonant between them that `syllabifyTokens` puts into one syllable count as one nucleus at the middle of the group. A nucleus is found when a reported one lies within 50 ms.\\n\\n- `finds the nuclei of synthetic speech`: over all sentences at least 90 % found and at most 10 % extra.\\n- `reports nuclei in time`: pushed in chunks of 20 ms, every nucleus is returned by `advance()` before 150 ms of later sound have been pushed, and the list returned by the stream equals the batch list within 10 ms.\\n- `names broad classes`: frame accuracy against the truth of step 7.2 at least 85 % at 16000 Hz and 80 % at 8000 Hz.\\n- `finds landmarks`: `apa`, `asa`, `ama`, `aba`: the closure, the release and the two voicing changes are each within 20 ms of the segment edge.\\n- `is quiet on noise`: 2 s of seeded white noise at -40 dB: no nucleus.\\n- `thresholds are up to date`: running the calibration on a tenth of its material gives thresholds within one search step of the committed ones.\\n- `stays within the CPU budget`: prints `landmarks rtf <x>`; fails above 0.08.\\n\\nHeavy test `tests/heavy/recognition-landmarks.test.js` (#14) on the golden set (#17): number of nuclei against the number of syllables of the IPA transcript (`syllabifyIpa`), per utterance.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] `node --test tests/recognition-landmarks.test.js` passes on Node.js, Bun and Deno.\\n- [ ] `node scripts/calibrate-landmarks.mjs` run twice writes the same file, byte for byte.\\n- [ ] `reports/heavy/recognition-landmarks.md` gives, for each golden utterance, the true and the found number of syllables, and the mean absolute relative error. Target to be measured, not a gate yet: 15 %.\\n- [ ] The CI log of `stays within the CPU budget` shows at most 0.02.\\n- [ ] `findLandmarks`, `createLandmarkDetector` and `BROAD_CLASSES` are exported and typed.\\n- [ ] `npm run check` and `npm test` pass; a minor changeset is added.\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n- Which vowel or consonant it is: #65, #66.\\n- Whispered speech, where rule 3.3 cannot hold: #72.\\n- Showing the nuclei in the app: #74.\\n- Stress and tone of a syllable: #70.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Mermelstein, P. (1975). Automatic segmentation of speech into syllabic units. JASA 58(4), 880-883. doi:10.1121/1.380738\\n- Liu, S. A. (1996). Landmark detection for distinctive feature-based speech recognition. JASA 100(5), 3417-3430. doi:10.1121/1.416983 (about 90 % of landmarks found and 6 to 9 % extra on clean read speech).\\n- Stevens, K. N. (2002). Toward a model for lexical access based on acoustic landmarks and distinctive features. JASA 111(4), 1872-1891. doi:10.1121/1.1458026\\n- de Jong, N. H., Wempe, T. (2009). Praat script to detect syllable nuclei and measure speech rate automatically. Behavior Research Methods 41(2), 385-390. doi:10.3758/brm.41.2.385\\n- Wang, D., Narayanan, S. S. (2007). Robust speech rate estimation for spontaneous speech. IEEE Trans. ASLP 15(8). doi:10.1109/tasl.2007.905178\\n- Juneja, A., Espy-Wilson, C. (2008). A probabilistic framework for landmark detection based on phonetic features for automatic speech recognition. JASA 123(2). doi:10.1121/1.2823754\\n- Research notes: `docs/case-studies/issue-11/research/2-inversion-and-recognition.md`.\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n**Blocked by** (must be closed before work starts):\\n\\n- #58 Streaming analysis front end\\n- #17 Golden set of public-domain recordings with transcripts and IPA\\n\\n**Blocks:**\\n\\n- #66 Decode articulation into IPA with times\\n\\n## Rules for every roadmap issue\\n\\n- Read [docs/VISION.md](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/VISION.md), [docs/ROADMAP.md](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/ROADMAP.md) and [docs/CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/CONTRIBUTING.md) first.\\n- **No neural networks** in the library, the app, the tests or the tools, and no playback of recorded speech. Allowed: physics, closed-form signal processing, search, optimisation, dynamic programming and small documented tables.\\n- **Public domain.** New code is Unlicense. New dependencies need a permissive licence (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0, ISC, Zlib, CC0, Unlicense). Committed data is public domain or CC0; anything else is downloaded at test time and never committed.\\n- One pull request per issue. Do not start before every issue under \\\"Blocked by\\\" is closed.\\n- Work in the JavaScript reference implementation in `src/` unless the issue says otherwise. If the directory `crates/` exists when you start, implement the same numerical code in the Rust core too and extend the parity tests.\\n- Same input, seed and options must give the same samples on Node.js, Bun and Deno. Take randomness only from `src/random.js`.\\n- Add tests in `tests/` that fail before the change and pass after it. Tests that need more than about 10 seconds or a download run only in the manually triggered workflow.\\n- Keep every source and Markdown file under 1500 lines, run `npm run check` and `npm test`, and add a changeset (`npm run changeset`) when code changes.\\n- Update `README.md`, `src/index.d.ts`, the command line help and `docs/ROADMAP.md` when behaviour or the public API changes. Put experiment scripts in `experiments/` and name them after this issue's number unless the issue gives a file name.\\n- Report measured numbers with the command that produced them. Do not write a claim that was not measured.\\n\",\"comments\":[],\"number\":62,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Syllable nuclei, landmarks and broad sound classes\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/issues/62\"}\n[]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
