[2026-11-17T11:56:19.786Z] [STDERR] The analysis front end of [#58](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/issues/58) (`src/recognition/`) resamples the input chunk by chunk to 16000, 11025 or 8000 Hz (`createResampler`, within 3e-8 of `resample()` for 44100 to 16000 Hz) and fills one frame set of flat `Float32Array`s every 5 or 10 ms: energy, zero crossings, centre of gravity, tilt, six Liu (1996) bands, periodicity (Boersma 1993), 24 mel energies, 13 cepstra, LPC by Burg, autocorrelation or covariance, a minimum-statistics noise floor (Martin 2001) and a speech flag. One `createFft` serves the whole package. The noise floor is capped at the loudest speech frame minus 25 dB, not the loudest frame, because a recording that starts with noise would otherwise hold the floor 25 dB under the noise and call all of it speech (`node experiments/issue-58-noise-cap.mjs`: 0 of 200 noise frames are called speech instead of 200 of 200). It takes 0.0138 of real time at 16000 Hz and 0.0059 at 8000 Hz and adds at most 13.5 ms of delay (`node experiments/issue-58-rtf.mjs`, `node experiments/issue-58-delay.mjs`). Stages #59 to #66 read the frame set and follow the `createX(frames, options)` / `advance(final)` / `committed` pattern (`FrameStage` in `src/recognition/frontend.d.ts`). The Rust core has the root finder (the LPC roots of the three orders, warm-started frame by frame, the dominant cost) and the tract length (`crates/voice-simulator-core/src/formants.rs`, export `vs_formant_candidates`), the same candidates as JavaScript in `tests/rust-parity.test.js`; the numbering, the smoothing and the use of the Rust engine by the tracker are left to [#77](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/issues/77).
