nd test, which flags 70 % of the nasal frames and 6.7 % of the vowel frames of the calibration set (`node experiments/issue-60-nasal.mjs`). The truth of the tests is `tubeFormants` of the planned area function, read on its 5 Hz grid by a 50 Hz scan and a hill climb (`gridFormants` in `tests/helpers/recognition-truth.js`, identical on 4350 frames and ten times faster, `node experiments/issue-60-truth.mjs`), so that `tests/recognition-formants.test.js` runs in about 30 s. The tests give the tract lengths 13.82, 17.24 and 19.43 cm for 13, 16.5 and 19 cm, a stream within 30 Hz of the batch in at least 99.6 % of the frames and 0.03 of real time at 16000 Hz. The audit targets (F1 within 5 %, F2 and F3 within 4 % of the planned formants) are not met: in [experiments/issue-11-audit-m4a.txt](../experiments/issue-11-audit-m4a.txt) `trackFormants` has mean errors of 7.2–17.9 % for F1, 4.2–5.6 % for F2 and 2.8–4.5 % for F3, and the `renderer formants` block shows that the rendered sound itself differs from the planned formants by 5.9–7.2 % in F1 and 5.0–5.6 % in F2, above the targets, so no tracker can meet them against that truth. On the golden set 99.6 % of the voiced frames have F1 to F3 and 0.4 % of the F2 steps jump by more than 300 Hz ([docs/reports/recognition-formants.md](reports/recognition-formants.md)); the VTR formant database is refused by the licence policy (`datasets/refused.json`), so no error per formant is measured on real speech. The Rust core has the root finder (the LPC roots of the three orders, warm-started frame by frame, the dominant cost) and the tract length (`crates/voice-simulator-core/src/formants.rs`, export `vs_formant_candidates`), the same candidates as JavaScript in `tests/rust-parity.test.js`; the numbering, the smoothing and the use of the Rust engine by the tracker are left to [#77](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/issues/77).\n \n-The draft implementation of [#62](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/issues/62)\n+The experime