imulator/milestone/5","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konard/voice-simulator/milestones/5/labels","id":19539682,"node_id":"MI_kwDOVMlws84BKibi","number":5,"title":"Roadmap 4: Reverse simulation (listening)","description":"Audio to IPA to language to text by inverting the simulation: analysis front end, inversion, decoding, language identification, API, app and benchmarks.","creator":{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard","html_url":"https://github.com/konard","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"open_issues":13,"closed_issues":7,"state":"open","created_at":"2026-11-14T17:54:15Z","updated_at":"2026-11-17T10:55:56Z","due_on":null,"closed_at":null},"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-11-14T17:57:24Z","updated_at":"2026-11-17T12:03:20Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","issue_field_values":[],"type":{"id":40096986,"node_id":"IT_kwDOABXZYM4CY9Ta","name":"Feature","description":"A request, idea, or new functionality","color":"blue","created_at":"2026-08-19T21:13:40Z","updated_at":"2026-08-19T21:13:40Z","is_enabled":true},"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":2,"blocking":1,"total_blocking":1},"body":"## Revised delivery contract (2026-11-17)\n\nFollowing the [owner's request for realistic requirements and a mergeable PR](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/pull/142#issuecomment-6947242499), PR #142 delivers the deterministic, experimental fixed-rule detector with the following explicit revisions. This section supersedes the conflicting timing rules and synthetic quality gates below; the original figures remain research goals, not claims of achieved accuracy. Every other requirement remains in scope.\n\n- A confirmed nucleus time is never retracted. Higher-peak replacement applies only to pending peaks; a confirmed peak wins over any later peak inside the 80 ms exclusion interval. Boundaries and strength may be completed as later minima arrive.\n- Streaming classes commit after 60 ms of analysis frames and stay fixed. Batch cleanup uses the whole recording. A silence run that is still open at commitment remains silence in the stream even if batch later calls it closure; exact class equality between the two modes is not promised. Nucleus times still agree between batch and 20 ms streams within 10 ms, with the 150 ms notification check retained.\n- Keep all four sentences, both presets and both rates, the original truth labels, matching tolerances and confusion matrices. Gate nucleus recall at **60%** and extras/truth at **35%**; gate class accuracy at **46% at 16 kHz / 45% at 8 kHz**. Current measured values are **63.3333%**, **33.3333%** and **48.2459% / 47.1143%**. These are regression limits for an experimental acoustic heuristic, not sufficient accuracy for transcription.\n- Keep the **20 ms** edge tolerance. Require at least **2/4 apa, 2/2 asa, 0/2 ama and 2/2 aba** planned edges (6/10 total), while printing every missing edge. Separately require every `g+`, `g-`, `s+`, `s-`, `b+`, `b-` event on controlled band transitions, with threshold and band-count rejection checks.\n- Require independent controlled tests for the complete class tree, voiced sonority peaks, noise/voicing rejection, cleanup, confirmed-peak priority and immutable streaming classes. No runtime job, parity suite or CI check is disabled or allowed to fail.\n- Preserve the original **90% recall / 10% extras**, **85% / 80% class accuracy**, **all planned edges**, and **15% golden MARE** as explicit quality goals. The first three original goals have not been reached. The golden report remains a measurement, with all 24 utterance counts and their review flags.\n\nThe [implementation contract](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/issue-62-c339234ca063/docs/RECOGNITION-LANDMARKS.md) and the [checked regression limits](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/issue-62-c339234ca063/tests/fixtures/landmark-quality.json) record these revisions. Calibration determinism, tenth-material stability, noise rejection, CPU budget, CLI, exports/types, changeset and Rust/WASM parity remain required.\n\n---\n\n## Original specification (quality goals retained for reference)\n\n> Part of the [roadmap](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/ROADMAP.md) planned in #11 (Roadmap 4: Reverse simulation (listening)). Type: **Feature**. Requirements of #11 covered: R6, R10, R12, R13, R18 (see the [case study](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/case-studies/issue-11/README.md)).\n\n## Goal\n\n`findLandmarks` reads the analysis frames and says three things without knowing the language: where each syllable has its centre (nucleus), where the sound changes abruptly (landmarks: a closure, a release, the start or end of voicing), and which broad class every frame belongs to (silence, vowel, glide, nasal, voiced fricative, voiceless fricative, closure, burst). In a stream a nucleus is reported at most 150 ms after it was spoken.\n\n## Why\n\n- Live highlighting (#74) must mark the syllable being spoken long before the words are known. Nuclei give that from energy alone.\n- The decoder (#66) needs places where a sound may begin or end, and a cheap first guess of the kind of sound, so that it compares each stretch with a few phonemes and not with all 97.\n- Speech rate for the voice fit (#63) is the number of nuclei per second.\n\n## Current state\n\n- `analyzeVoice` gives one number, `syllableRate`: peaks of a smoothed energy curve of the whole recording. The peak times are not returned and nothing works on a stream.\n- No code finds closures, bursts or the start of voicing, and no code names the kind of sound of a frame.\n- The forward direction knows all of this exactly: `planUtterance` returns `plan.segments` with `kind` (`pause`, `consonant`, `release`, `vowel`), `start` and `end`, and the tokens carry manner and voicing (`src/articulation/phones.js`, `MANNERS`). So the simulator can make as much labelled speech as needed to set thresholds.\n- `syllabifyIpa(ipa, {language})` in `src/ipa/syllables.js` gives the true number of syllables of a transcript.\n\n## What to do\n\nCreate `src/recognition/landmarks.js` (stage pattern of #58) and the script `scripts/calibrate-landmarks.mjs`. Inputs per frame, all from the frame set: `energy`, `noise`, `speech`, `periodicity`, `zcr`, `gravity`, `tilt`, `band` (6 values) and `mel`.\n\n1. Exported constant `BROAD_CLASSES`, a list of names in this order (the index is the class number): `silence`, `vowel`, `glide`, `nasal`, `voiced-fricative`, `voiceless-fricative`, `closure`, `burst`.\n2. Sonority curve `s[t]` in dB: `10 * log10` of the sum of `exp(mel[m])` over the filters with centres from 300 to 3000 Hz (to 2800 Hz when `rate` is 8000), smoothed with a moving mean of 40 ms. Store it as `sonority`.\n3. Nuclei (after Mermelstein 1975; de Jong and Wempe 2009). Frame `t` is a nucleus when all hold:\n   1. `s[t]` is the largest value within 40 ms on both sides;\n   2. `s[t] >= noise[t] + NUCLEUS_ABOVE_NOISE` (start value 10 dB);\n   3. `periodicity[t] >= 0.45` (voiced);\n   4. between `t` and the nucleus before it `s` falls at least `NUCLEUS_DIP` (start value 2 dB) below the lower of the two peaks;\n   5. the nucleus before it is at least 80 ms away. If rule 4 or 5 fails, keep the higher of the two peaks only.\n\n   A nucleus is confirmed, and never taken back, when `s` has fallen `NUCLEUS_DIP` below it or when 100 ms have passed, whichever comes first; with the 40 ms of rule 1 the delay is at most 150 ms. Each nucleus is `{time, start, end, strength}`: `strength` = `s[t]` minus the lower of the two minima around it; `start` and `end` = the minima of `s` between neighbouring nuclei, or the nearest frame with `speech` 0. `end` is `null` until the next minimum is known.\n\n4. Landmarks (after Liu 1996). For each of the six bands compute the rise `r_b[t] = band_b[t + d] - band_b[t - d]` with `d` = 25 ms (coarse) and 13 ms (fine). A peak of `|r_b|` above `LANDMARK_RISE` (start value 9 dB) in the coarse pass, placed at the nearest peak of the fine pass within 20 ms, is a candidate. Types:\n   - `g+` / `g-`: band 0 (0 to 400 Hz) rises or falls and `periodicity` crosses 0.45: voicing starts or stops.\n   - `s+` / `s-`: inside a voiced stretch, at least three of bands 1 to 4 rise or fall together: a sonorant consonant ends or begins.\n   - `b+` / `b-`: outside a voiced stretch, at least three of bands 1 to 5 rise or fall together: a burst or frication starts or stops.\n\n   Each landmark is `{time, type, strength}` (`strength` = mean `|r_b|` in dB over the bands that took part). Two landmarks of one type closer than 20 ms: keep the stronger.\n\n5. Broad class of frame `t`, a fixed tree; `e = energy - noise`:\n   1. `speech` is 0 and `e < T.silence` : silence (or closure, see step 6).\n   2. `periodicity < T.voiced`: if the frame is within 20 ms after a `b+` landmark and the frame 20 ms earlier was silence or closure: burst; otherwise voiceless fricative.\n   3. voiced, and `zcr > T.fricativeZcr` or `gravity > T.fricativeGravity`: voiced fricative.\n   4. voiced, `band[0] - band[2] > T.nasalDrop` and `s[t] < peak - T.nasalBelowPeak`: nasal. `peak` = the largest `s` from 200 ms before the frame to 40 ms after it.\n   5. voiced and `s[t] < peak - T.glideBelowPeak`: glide (this class also holds liquids).\n   6. otherwise vowel.\n\n   In a stream the class of a frame is fixed 60 ms after the frame (40 ms for `peak`, 20 ms for the clean-up of step 6); `committed` counts these.\n\n6. Clean-up: a median over 5 frames of the class numbers; a silence run shorter than 150 ms with speech on both sides becomes closure; a run of any class shorter than 15 ms takes the class of the longer neighbour (bursts may be 5 ms).\n7. `scripts/calibrate-landmarks.mjs` finds the thresholds `T` and the three start values above. It must be deterministic and is run by hand:\n   1. Render with `synthesize` (`solver: 'waveguide'`, `seed: 1`) every consonant of `src/ipa/inventory.js` between the vowels `a`, `i`, `u` (`aCa`, `iCi`, `uCu`), for the presets male, female and child, at 8000, 11025 and 16000 Hz, clean and with seeded white noise 20 dB below the speech.\n   2. Truth per frame from `plan.segments` and the token: [REDACTED] is silence; vowel is vowel; a consonant by manner: plosive closure part is closure, its `release` segment burst; nasal is nasal; fricatives by voicing; approximants, laterals, taps and trills are glide. Frames within 10 ms of a segment edge are not counted.\n   3. Coordinate search: for each threshold in turn try 21 values across its allowed range, keep the one with the highest mean of the per-class recall; three rounds.\n   4. Write `src/recognition/landmark-thresholds.js` (one exported object, with the reached recall per class and per rate in a comment).\n8. Result: an object with `hop`, `startTime`, `count`, `committed`, `classes`, `sonority`, `landmarks` and `nuclei`; `classes` is a `Uint8Array`, `sonority` a `Float32Array`, both with `frames.capacity` values; `landmarks` and `nuclei` are arrays that only grow. Exports: `findLandmarks(frames, options)`, `BROAD_CLASSES` and `createLandmarkDetector(frames, options)` (its `advance()` returns the nuclei confirmed by this call).\n\nBudget on the reference machine of #58: at most 2 % of real time.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew file `tests/recognition-landmarks.test.js`. Sentences: \"The north wind and the sun were disputing which was the stronger.\" (English), \"Мама мыла раму.\" (Russian), \"नमस्ते दुनिया\" (Hindi), \"你好世界\" (Mandarin); presets male and female; 16000 and 8000 Hz; `lang` set for each. True nuclei = the middle of each vowel segment of `plan.segments`; vowel segments with no consonant between them that `syllabifyTokens` puts into one syllable count as one nucleus at the middle of the group. A nucleus is found when a reported one lies within 50 ms.\n\n- `finds the nuclei of synthetic speech`: over all sentences at least 90 % found and at most 10 % extra.\n- `reports nuclei in time`: pushed in chunks of 20 ms, every nucleus is returned by `advance()` before 150 ms of later sound have been pushed, and the list returned by the stream equals the batch list within 10 ms.\n- `names broad classes`: frame accuracy against the truth of step 7.2 at least 85 % at 16000 Hz and 80 % at 8000 Hz.\n- `finds landmarks`: `apa`, `asa`, `ama`, `aba`: the closure, the release and the two voicing changes are each within 20 ms of the segment edge.\n- `is quiet on noise`: 2 s of seeded white noise at -40 dB: no nucleus.\n- `thresholds are up to date`: running the calibration on a tenth of its material gives thresholds within one search step of the committed ones.\n- `stays within the CPU budget`: prints `landmarks rtf <x>`; fails above 0.08.\n\nHeavy test `tests/heavy/recognition-landmarks.test.js` (#14) on the golden set (#17): number of nuclei against the number of syllables of the IPA transcript (`syllabifyIpa`), per utterance.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `node --test tests/recognition-landmarks.test.js` passes on Node.js, Bun and Deno.\n- [ ] `node scripts/calibrate-landmarks.mjs` run twice writes the same file, byte for byte.\n- [ ] `reports/heavy/recognition-landmarks.md` gives, for each golden utterance, the true and the found number of syllables, and the mean absolute relative error. Target to be measured, not a gate yet: 15 %.\n- [ ] The CI log of `stays within the CPU budget` shows at most 0.02.\n- [ ] `findLandmarks`, `createLandmarkDetector` and `BROAD_CLASSES` are exported and typed.\n- [ ] `npm run check` and `npm test` pass; a minor changeset is added.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Which vowel or consonant it is: #65, #66.\n- Whispered speech, where rule 3.3 cannot hold: #72.\n- Showing the nuclei in the app: #74.\n- Stress and tone of a syllable: #70.\n\n## References\n\n- Mermelstein, P. (1975). Automatic segmentation of speech into syllabic units. JASA 58(4), 880-883. doi:10.1121/1.380738\n- Liu, S. A. (1996). Landmark detection for distinctive feature-based speech recognition. JASA 100(5), 3417-3430. doi:10.1121/1.416983 (about 90 % of landmarks found and 6 to 9 % extra on clean read speech).\n- Stevens, K. N. (2002). Toward a model for lexical access based on acoustic landmarks and distinctive features. JASA 111(4), 1872-1891. doi:10.1121/1.1458026\n- de Jong, N. H., Wempe, T. (2009). Praat script to detect syllable nuclei and measure speech rate automatically. Behavior Research Methods 41(2), 385-390. doi:10.3758/brm.41.2.385\n- Wang, D., Narayanan, S. S. (2007). Robust speech rate estimation for spontaneous speech. IEEE Trans. ASLP 15(8). doi:10.1109/tasl.2007.905178\n- Juneja, A., Espy-Wilson, C. (2008). A probabilistic framework for landmark detection based on phonetic features for automatic speech recognition. JASA 123(2). doi:10.1121/1.2823754\n- Research notes: `docs/case-studies/issue-11/research/2-inversion-and-recognition.md`.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n**Blocked by** (must be closed before work starts):\n\n- #58 Streaming analysis front end\n- #17 Golden set of public-domain recordings with transcripts and IPA\n\n**Blocks:**\n\n- #66 Decode articulation into IPA with times\n\n## Rules for every roadmap issue\n\n- Read [docs/VISION.md](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/VISION.md), [docs/ROADMAP.md](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/ROADMAP.md) and [docs/CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/main/docs/CONTRIBUTING.md) first.\n- **No neural networks** in the library, the app, the tests or the tools, and no playback of recorded speech. Allowed: physics, closed-form signal processing, search, optimisation, dynamic programming and small documented tables.\n- **Public domain.** New code is Unlicense. New dependencies need a permissive licence (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0, ISC, Zlib, CC0, Unlicense). Committed data is public domain or CC0; anything else is downloaded at test time and never committed.\n- One pull request per issue. Do not start before every issue under \"Blocked by\" is closed.\n- Work in the JavaScript reference implementation in `src/` unless the issue says otherwise. If the directory `crates/` exists when you start, implement the same numerical code in the Rust core too and extend the parity tests.\n- Same input, seed and options must give the same samples on Node.js, Bun and Deno. Take randomness only from `src/random.js`.\n- Add tests in `tests/` that fail before the change and pass after it. Tests that need more than about 10 seconds or a download run only in the manually triggered workflow.\n- Keep every source and Markdown file under 1500 lines, run `npm run check` and `npm test`, and add a changeset (`npm run changeset`) when code changes.\n- Update `README.md`, `src/index.d.ts`, the command line help and `docs/ROADMAP.md` when behaviour or the public API changes. Put experiment scripts in `experiments/` and name them after this issue's number unless the issue gives a file name.\n- Report measured numbers with the command that produced them. Do not write a claim that was not measured.\n\n","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konard/voice-simulator/issues/62/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konard/voice-simulator/issues/62/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}[[]]{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konard/voice-simulator/pulls/142","id":5351855759,"node_id":"PR_kwDOVMlws88AAAABPv7Wjw","html_url":"https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/pull/142","diff_url":"https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/pull/142.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/pull/142.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/konard/voice-simulator/issues/142","number":142,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"Add experimental syllable nuclei, landmarks and broad classes","user":{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard","html_url":"https://github.com/konard","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Add experimental syllable nuclei, acoustic landmarks and eight broad sound classes from the existing analysis frames, with batch/stream APIs and a JSON CLI command.\n\nFixes #62\n\nFollowing [the owner's request for realistic requirements and a mergeable implementation](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/pull/142#issuecomment-6947242499), the [issue](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/issues/62) and [canonical plan](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/issue-62-c339234ca063/docs/case-studies/issue-11/plan/P50-syllable-nuclei-landmarks-broad-classes.md) now specify a consistent experimental contract. Original quality goals, measurements and counterexamples remain documented. The detector's recognition accuracy has not increased in this revision.\n\n- Reuse mel energies, periodicity and six analysis bands for sonority, voiced peaks, two-scale signed landmarks and the calibrated class tree. Add public exports/types, configurable thresholds, CLI/help, a golden report and a minor release changeset. No new dependency or spectrum computation.\n- Keep confirmed nucleus times immutable; only pending peaks can be replaced by a later higher peak inside 80 ms. Keep the exact 60 ms immutable class prefix. Batch can retrospectively label a bounded silence as closure; streaming retains labels already committed before that silence ends. Flush/finalization drains pending output.\n- Preserve the original multilingual inputs, truth and tolerances, and assert explicit measured regression limits. Add independent analytic tests for all six landmark signs, grouping/rejection, peaks, every class-tree branch and cleanup. No runtime job is disabled or allowed to fail.\n- Port the numerical batch detector to Rust with an additive WASM export. Preserve all merged acoustics, source-analysis, formant, codebook, IPA and language APIs and parity suites. The combined scalar engine is 467,028 bytes, within the measured 475,136-byte budget.\n- Fix earlier CI-discovered streaming suppression/strength regressions and anatomy-download cancellation races with focused reproducing tests. Optional archive preparation has a bounded transport budget; malformed archives and programming errors still fail.\n\n### Quality measurements and acceptance limits\n\n| Measurement | Observed | Revised regression limit | Original goal |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Nucleus recall within 50 ms | 76/120 (63.3333%) | ≥60% | ≥90% |\n| Extras / true nuclei | 40/120 (33.3333%) | ≤35% | ≤10% |\n| Class accuracy, 16 kHz | 48.2459% | ≥46% | ≥85% |\n| Class accuracy, 8 kHz | 47.1143% | ≥45% | ≥80% |\n| Planned edges within 20 ms | 6/10 | 2 apa + 2 asa + 0 ama + 2 aba | 10/10 |\n| Golden syllable-count MARE | 24.41% | Measured only | ≤15% |\n\nThe four-language evaluation retains both voices and both rates. Tests print the confusion matrices and all missing edges. [Versioned limits](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/issue-62-c339234ca063/tests/fixtures/landmark-quality.json) and the [API guide](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/issue-62-c339234ca063/docs/RECOGNITION-LANDMARKS.md) make these limits explicit. These are acoustic guesses and should not unconditionally exclude downstream phoneme candidates. The [golden report](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/issue-62-c339234ca063/docs/case-studies/issue-62/golden-report.md) includes all 24 utterances and their pending human-review flags.\n\n### Reproduction and automated verification\n\n```sh\nnode bin/voice-simulator.js landmarks recording.wav -o landmarks.json\nnode --test --test-timeout=30000 tests/recognition-landmarks.test.js tests/recognition-landmark-rules.test.js tests/recognition-landmark-streaming.test.js\nREQUIRE_WASM=1 node --test tests/rust-landmarks.test.js tests/recognition-landmark-boundaries.test.js tests/engine-size.test.js\nnode --test tests/heavy/recognition-landmarks.test.js\nnode scripts/calibrate-landmarks.mjs --out /tmp/landmark-thresholds.js\n```\n\nBefore this acceptance revision, the nine runtime jobs all failed the same three incompatible/overstated quality assertions in [run 44071097877](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/actions/runs/44071097877). The original candidate set can cover at most 98/120 reference centres before filtering, and actual PCM demonstrates the conflict between retrospective closure cleanup and immutable 60 ms classes. Those finite experiments are retained in `experiments/issue-62/`.\n\nThe preserved full log (`gh run view 44071097877 --repo konard/voice-simulator --log`) shows the Ubuntu Node failures at lines 15136, 15148 and 15162: nucleus recall, class accuracy and missing `apa`/`ama` edges respectively. Their assertion sites were `recognition-landmarks.test.js:174`, `:247` and `:280`. The updated suite retains those measurements and uses the revised, versioned acceptance limits alongside independent signal-rule checks.\n\nLocal validation on 2026-11-17:\n\n- Node.js, Bun and Deno: 22 focused rule, quality and streaming checks each pass. The immutable-prefix test uses two real PCM recordings with the same prefix but different later closure outcomes. The confirmed-peak test checks identity/time after a higher nearby peak arrives.\n- Eight additional Node checks pass for nucleus boundaries, engine size and JS/WASM parity across three rates, two voices and both hops; CLI JSON/file output passes. The existing CI-built WASM matches the unchanged numerical source.\n- Both heavy landmark tests pass. All 24 golden rows reproduce the committed report exactly; tenth-material calibration stays within one search step. Two complete 3780-render calibrations reproduce the committed threshold module byte for byte after formatting.\n- `npm run check`, `cargo fmt --all --check` and `git diff --check` pass.\n\n[Complete implementation CI 44080857443](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/actions/runs/44080857443) passes on **`0e907a9802e926d7ce4f9edb7ce91e86dec716c1`**: all nine Node.js/Bun/Deno × Linux/macOS/Windows jobs, Rust workspace/build/clippy/parity, lint/format/policy, documentation, simulation, compilation, file limits and release checks. The Rust job passes 187 parity checks and seven thread tests without skips. Every runtime reports the same quality measurements; landmark CPU RTF ranges from 0.000127 to 0.000978, below 0.02. Example app, Security, Workflows and Broken Link Checker also pass on this SHA.\n\nTwo complete formatted calibration outputs and the committed module share SHA-256 `01e01bce0d21594143f2fef34dcda058430a1cac740526f2e7f9fb45356b1203`. Latest `main` (`eeb2869`) is merged. The PR is ready for review and remains unmerged. [Final documentation CI 44083260725](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/actions/runs/44083260725) also passes at **`a2b6a20ffb2da5ae85afd979169fc3843c321cc1`**; all four companion workflows pass on this SHA, including [desktop packaging](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/actions/runs/44083260380).\n\nThe [case study](https://github.com/konard/voice-simulator/blob/issue-62-c339234ca063/docs/case-studies/issue-62/README.md) maps every delivered requirement to implementation and verification, and retains the historical investigation.\n","created_at":"2026-11-17T06:13:29Z","updated_at":"2026-11-17T12:41:14Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"82d5154fc1219d3a17eac6fc6092408fb9c8bffd","assignees":[{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard","html_url":"https://github.com/konard","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/priva